<<<
highly. 1. It should be remembered that high is an adv. as well as highly, & better in many contexts; c.g., It is best to pay your men high; Migh-placed officials; see UNIDIOM- ATIC -Ly.
===
**highly.** 1. It should be remembered that *high* is an adv. as well as *highly*, & better in many contexts; e.g., *It is best to pay your men high*; *High-placed officials*; see ^^UNIDIOMATIC -LY^^.
>>>

<<<
2. Though highly in the sense to a high degree is often unobjectionable (a highly contentious question; highly farmed land), it has acquired,
===
2. Though *highly* in the sense *to a high degree* is often unobjectionable (*a highly contentious question; highly farmed land*), it has acquired,
>>>

<<<
a patronizing taint (a highly entertaining performance) like distinctly, & is best avoided in such connexions.
